KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ handler sees the thrown value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [except.throw]/3-4, [except.handle]/1-3,15: throwing transfers control to
the matching handler and initializes the handler parameter from the exception
object, so `try { throw 42; } catch(int e)` enters the handler with e == 42.

CBMC does not model C++ exception propagation: goto-symex's symex_throw is
stubbed to `assume(false)` and symex_catch is a no-op (both #if 0'd out, TODO
TG-4667).  The thrown value never reaches the handler and the handler body is
effectively unreachable, so both `e == 42` and `e != 42` are vacuously provable
(VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL) -- masking any bug in a catch handler.

The C++ front-end emits CATCH-PUSH/CATCH-POP/THROW instructions (which symex
stubs); a separate $exception_flag+goto mechanism (convert_CPROVER_try_catch /
convert_CPROVER_throw) models control flow but matches neither the exception
type nor binds the value.  A conforming model requires type-aware handler
matching, binding the handler parameter to the thrown value, control transfer
to the matching handler, stack unwinding and destructor calls during unwinding
(implemented in goto-symex symex_throw/symex_catch, or as a goto-level
exception-lowering pass).  Flip to CORE once the thrown value reaches the
matching handler.
